The repair cafe needs a new sign. The previous version has the correct
accessibility information and the wrong closing time. You want to
change one thing, not accidentally bury yesterday's decision about the
entrance. Transformations make the candidate; comparisons establish
whether that candidate is ready to leave the practice folder.

\begin{objectives}
\item Replace or remove text in a stream without modifying the original.
\item Read a unified diff and distinguish differences from command errors.
\item Create, review and verify a separate candidate before distributing it.
\end{objectives}
\IndexCommand{sed}
\IndexCommand{tr}
\IndexCommand{diff}
\IndexTerm{preview before saving}

\section{Make a trial change}
Use \cmd|sh| for the practice syntax. Run the first two lines
separately and stop if either fails. The fixture includes an access
note that must survive the change.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp22.XXXXXX)
cd "$practice"
printf '%s\n' 'Repair cafe' 'Close: 5' 'Accessible entrance' \
  'Ask about Bay 5' > sign.txt
\end{terminal}

\cmd|sed| reads input, applies editing instructions and by
default prints the resulting lines. Applying an edit to a
stream is not the same as saving an edit to a file. The
following commands change what goes to standard output, with
no redirection and no special in-place option.

\begin{terminal}
sed 's/5/6/' sign.txt
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
Repair cafe
Close: 6
Accessible entrance
Ask about Bay 6
\end{terminaloutput}

The command beginning with \texttt{s} is a substitution.
The slash after \texttt{s} separates its parts: first is
the search pattern, then the replacement, and the final slash
ends the replacement. Each line with a match loses its
first match to the replacement. That still changes two
lines. The utility interpreted the rule exactly as written;
our rule forgot to say which 5.

\begin{terminal}
sed 's/^Close: 5$/Close: 6/' sign.txt
cat sign.txt
\end{terminal}

The anchors make the whole old closing line the target.
The first command shows a new closing time and the old bay
number. The second shows the unchanged original. If the
original instead said \texttt{Close: 5 pm}, the new rule would
leave it alone. Treat an unchanged candidate as a reason
to inspect the file rather than weaken the rule blindly:
the apparent discrepancy could be whitespace, punctuation,
or the wrong working directory. A substitution that doesn't
match usually completes successfully.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-22/substitute.tikz}
\caption{The closing line matches the anchored rule; the bay line does not.}
\label{fig:22-substitute}
\VisualDescription{Close: 5 passes the whole-line test and becomes Close: 6. Ask about Bay 5 fails the test and remains unchanged.}
\end{figure}

\section{Distinguish a match from its replacement}
Within the pattern of a substitution, sed normally uses
basic regular expressions. In the replacement part, an
unescaped ampersand means the entire matched text. This is
handy when the matched text should remain visible with a
new label. Appending \texttt{g} after the closing slash
changes each non-overlapping match in the current line, not
just the first. It does not mean recurse through files.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Bay 5, Gate 5' | sed 's/5/[&]/'
printf '%s\n' 'Bay 5, Gate 5' | sed 's/5/[&]/g'
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{Bay [5], Gate 5} and then
\texttt{Bay [5], Gate [5]}. Here the brackets in the
replacement are ordinary characters. In the search pattern,
they would usually introduce a character choice. If you use
literal slash or ampersand in a replacement, deal with the
substitution grammar as well as the shell grammar. Don't
paste an arbitrary clipboard string into the middle of an
editing rule and assume it will be read as literal text.

\begin{terminal}
sed -n '/^Close:/p' sign.txt
\end{terminal}

\cmd|-n| stops the default printing. The expression between
the first two slashes is an address that selects matching
lines, and \texttt{p} prints those lines explicitly. The
result is just \texttt{Close: 5}. Without \cmd|-n| the
explicitly printed line would be printed in addition to the
default print. A selector like this does not replace text,
and the editing address is not a shell filename pattern.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.24in}Y}
\toprule
Construction & Effect in these examples \\
\midrule
\texttt{s/old/new/} & Replace the first match in each input line. \\
\texttt{s/old/new/g} & Replace each non-overlapping match in each input line. \\
\texttt{\&} in replacement & Insert the portion matched by the pattern. \\
\texttt{-n} with \texttt{/pattern/p} & Print only lines selected by the address; no substitution required. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Translate characters, not words}
Use \cmd|tr| when the operation is on individual characters.
It reads standard input rather than taking a file operand.
Two sets specify the source characters and their corresponding
replacements. An ASCII fixture can use the standard character
classes to translate uppercase letters to lowercase in the
C locale.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'GATE ACCESS' |
  LC_ALL=C tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{gate access}. The single quotes preserve the
arguments for \cmd|tr| to read; these are its own sets, not
grep expressions. When you use a range or class, think about
which locale applies and whether your implementation handles
the intended characters. Do not use this C-locale recipe as
a general Unicode case converter for names. It gives a
predictable ASCII example, not a language-aware spelling rule.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'A--B----C' | tr -s '-'
printf '%s\n' 'rooms 5 and 6' | tr -d '0123456789'
\end{terminal}

The first command squeezes adjacent copies of a listed
character to one. A bare hyphen operand is not an option
here; it denotes the hyphen to squeeze. The result is
\texttt{A-B-C}. The second deletes all occurrences of the
listed digits; it leaves the spaces, even where there
are now two adjacent spaces. These transformations remove
information. In a public label it might be fine to collapse
accidental repeated punctuation; in an identifier, the
same change might turn distinct codes into the same code.
First check why the character is there.

\section{Compare before you keep}
\label{sec:22-diff}
Use a distinct new filename for the candidate. The shell
opens the file named after \cmd|>| before sed reads the
input. If you accidentally use \texttt{sign.txt} on both
sides, it can be truncated before sed reads anything.
Even a different-looking name might refer to the same file
through a link. In this fresh practice directory the new
name is unused; on real files, check that you have a truly
new destination and a backup before you write.

\begin{terminal}
sed 's/^Close: 5$/Close: 6/' sign.txt > sign-candidate.txt
diff -u sign.txt sign-candidate.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The \cmd|-u| option requests unified format: additions and
deletions are shown in one view along with context lines.
The first operand is your old version; the second is the
candidate. Here is the body of the difference; the omitted
file headers normally include paths and may include timestamps:

\begin{terminaloutput}
@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@
 Repair cafe
-Close: 5
+Close: 6
 Accessible entrance
 Ask about Bay 5
\end{terminaloutput}

A hunk is one contiguous group of changes and the surrounding
context. The marker line says there are four lines starting
at line 1 in the old file and four starting at line 1 in
the new file. A leading minus marks a line removed from
the old version; a leading plus marks an addition in the
candidate. A leading space marks context, unchanged around
the edit. None of those leading markers is in the actual
sign text. The sign's access note and bay number were printed
precisely so you can see that they stayed in place.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-22/hunk.tikz}
\caption{Read a changed line alongside its unchanged neighbors.}
\label{fig:22-hunk}
\VisualDescription{Old and new closing lines are marked with minus and plus respectively. A space marks the unchanged entrance note.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.25in}Y}
\toprule
Exit status & Reading for diff \\
\midrule
0 & No differences found. Check the transformation if a change was expected. \\
1 & Differences found. Review whether they match your intent. \\
Greater than 1 & The comparison encountered an error. Read standard error and check both input paths. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Here the status is 1 because the files differ. Not all
nonzero statuses represent failures: in a script that is
supposed to find a change, this one can be the desired
result. Conversely, an identical file returns zero even
if the change was essential. For comparing exact bytes
without asking which lines changed, use \cmd|cmp| as in
Chapter~\ref{ch:7}. A unified diff is primarily a review of
changes you can read, not a method for deciding all kinds
of equivalence.

\begin{terminal}
diff -u sign.txt sign.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

The comparison produces nothing and its status is zero.
To turn an approved candidate into a distribution copy,
make an explicitly named new file and compare it with what
you approved. Keep the original and the candidate in this
exercise; making a third copy is for practicing the
boundary where a reviewed version becomes a deliverable.

\begin{terminal}
cp -i sign-candidate.txt sign-for-print.txt
diff -u sign-candidate.txt sign-for-print.txt
\end{terminal}

\cmd|cp -i| asks before overwriting an existing destination;
decline if you weren't expecting the name to exist. The
second command should show no differences. If you need to
undo a rejected candidate, leave the original alone and
remove only the candidate after verifying the path. If
the original has already been overwritten, an editor's undo
buffer or a backup might help, but a shell redirection has
no built-in undo.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-22/review.tikz}
\caption{A separate output file is a place to stop and inspect before publication.}
\label{fig:22-review}
\VisualDescription{An arrow runs from the original to a candidate, then through a review decision to a print copy. The original remains separate.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{The command worked, but did the edit?}
\Brian{Diff returned 1. Does that mean my file is broken?}
\Stephen{No, 1 means the two contents differ.}
\Brian{But if I ask for an edit and see no difference, diff returns zero.}
\Stephen{Right. Zero answers its question, not whether your edit succeeded.}
\Brian{And the first edit changed the bay. That would also return 1.}
\Stephen{Exactly. Read the changes; the status doesn't say they were intended.}
\Brian{I'll let the status tell me where to look, not what to approve.}
\DialogueConclusion{Review the differences, not just the status.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\begin{platformnote}{GNU and BSD implementations}
The examples use streaming edits. Do not copy a recipe with
\texttt{sed -i} into work you care about: in-place editing
is not a POSIX option, and GNU and BSD implementations differ
in the way the backup suffix is supplied. Consult the local
\texttt{man sed}, especially if switching between Linux and
macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD or NetBSD. Keep in-place edits out of
the preview phase on any system.
\end{platformnote}

\begin{furtherreading}
\ReadingItem{POSIX sed}{The substitution command, addresses and stream output}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/sed.html}
\ReadingItem{POSIX tr}{Translation, deletion and squeezing}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/tr.html}
\ReadingItem{POSIX diff}{Unified format and exit status}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/diff.html}
\end{furtherreading}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Make a transform narrow enough to protect unrelated text.
\item Keep the source untouched while you create a candidate.
\item Read the diff and verify the final copy as its own step.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Keep the bay number}
\label{ex:22-bay}
Preview a closing time of 7, leaving the bay at 5. Expect four
output lines, with only the closing line changed. Do not redirect
this preview; there is nothing new to clean up.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sed 's/^Close: 5$/Close: 7/' sign.txt
\end{terminal}
The line about the bay should still contain 5.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Practice a deletion on synthetic text}
\label{ex:22-delete}
Remove colons from the input \texttt{A:B:C}. Expect
\texttt{ABC}. Use a pipeline and no output file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'A:B:C' | tr -d ':'
\end{terminal}
With no saved output, no exercise cleanup is needed.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Turn the comparison around}
\label{ex:22-reverse}
Compare the saved candidate to the original, in that order.
Expect the signs on the two changed closing lines to be
reversed compared with the earlier diff. Keep both files for
the chapter cleanup.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
diff -u sign-candidate.txt sign.txt
\end{terminal}
Expect a removal of the 6 line and an addition of the 5 line.
\end{solution}

\paragraph{Cleanup.}
Verify \cmd|pwd| against the path stored in \cmd|"$practice"|.
In that disposable \texttt{utp22} directory only, remove
the named files; decline any prompt you weren't expecting.
Missing-file errors are harmless if you skipped a step.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./sign.txt ./sign-candidate.txt ./sign-for-print.txt
cd /tmp
rmdir "$practice"
\end{terminal}

Inspect any unexpected leftover rather than forcing directory
removal. If you started a separate \cmd|sh|, type \cmd|exit|.
